    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d443">

                <pb facs="0435" n="423" sameAs="frus:frus1944v05/pg423"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">891.20 Mission/8–344: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Iran</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Ford</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tehran</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-08-03T09:00:00+03:25">August 3, 1944—9
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received August 4—5:36 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>567. Re Depts No. 407 dated July 4. Iranian Government has formally requested
                    that American Military Mission headed by General <pb facs="0436" n="424"
                        xml:id="pg424"/> Ridley be retained here for period of 1 year from November
                    1, 1944. Reason given for desiring this extension is that mission has certain
                    useful works under way which are important to Iran and which cannot be completed
                    within some months’ time. Government further avows its complete satisfaction
                    with work of mission and requests necessary steps be taken with War Department
                    to have extensions approved.</p>
                <p>General Ridley consulted regarding this request feels that if any concession is
                    made to Iranian Government it should not extend beyond March 1, 1945 because by
                    that time all useful work commensurate with his effort will be completed.</p>
                <p>While reason advanced by Iranian Government is not particularly compelling one in
                    face of War Department’s stand I nevertheless feel our interests in Iran would
                    be materially served by having mission remain long enough to complete its work
                    and accordingly urge Department’s active support vis-à-vis War Department of
                    compromise suggested by Ridley. He and I concur that should this 4–month
                    extension be granted Iranian Government should be advised now of date when
                    mission will be withdrawn.<anchor n="27" xml:id="d443-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ford</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d443-f1" n="27" xml:id="d443-f1-content">
                <p>In a letter of August 14 the Secretary of State conveyed this information to the
                    Secretary of War and recommended that the Ridley Military Mission continue its
                    services in Iran until March 1, 1945 (891.20 Mission/17).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
